  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 344 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nRain from the early morning has gradually progressed east and exited \nthe forecast area, leaving behind ample sunshine and warming \ntemperatures under clear skies. This has allowed temperatures to \nclimb into the 80s through the afternoon, with only those areas that \nremain under dense cloud cover struggling to breach the low/mid 70s. \nAs high pressure continues sliding off towards the east, winds \nremain out of the east/southeast, with occasional gusts up to \n20 kts as daytime heating promotes mixing in the low-levels. \nPleasant weather will persist through the overnight hours, where\ntemperatures will drop to the 60s as winds grow light. \n\nShortwave disturbances are forecast to continue propagating along \nthe periphery of the upper-level ridge into tomorrow, continuing \nopportunities for precipitation into late tomorrow. At present, CAMs \nhave been less enthusiastic about the potential for robust activity \nreaching eastern Kansas/western Missouri. Instead, CAMs have \nconsistently simulated a bulk of activity further west, with storm \ncomplexes simulated developing off of these disturbances in an \nenvironment with adequate shear ~30-35 kts and MLCAPE ~2500-3000 \nJ/kg coupled with steep low/mid-level lapse rates. If activity does \nreach eastern Kansas and western Missouri, it appears to be more \ndisorganized and weaker in nature. However, with adequate MLCAPE and \nmid-level lapse rates ~7 degC/km, strong/severe elevated \nthunderstorms with robust updrafts are not out of the question in \nthe evening/overnight. With this activity, the primary hazards would \nbe damaging winds/large hail, focused across northwestern Missouri \nand northeastern Kansas closer to the shortwave disturbance.  \n\nThe forecast looks to dry out after this activity going into \nWednesday, with temperatures remaining seasonable under retreating \ncloud cover and low relative humidity. The upper-level trough \ncurrently situated over the northern Plains is forecast to dig \ntowards the Great Lakes through midweek, draping a cold front across \nthe forecast area and bringing maximum temperatures down by a \nfew degrees by Thursday. There is an additional opportunity for \nprecipitation to develop along the leading edge of the front, \nbut guidance favors later development along the front during the\nday Thursday, which would place it further east and south of \nthe forecast area. The upper-level ridge situated over the \nsouthwestern CONUS will push further into the central CONUS, and\nwill place the forecast area beyond the periphery of the upper-\nlevel ridge, keeping weather dry through the weekend. However, \ngreater model divergence at this time leaves some uncertainty on\nwhen precipitation will be reintroduced to the forecast with \nthe potential deamplification of the upper-level ridge over the \ncentral CONUS. Winds shifting southerly by the end of the \nforecast period with surface high pressure situated over the \neastern CONUS will lead to increasing temperatures into the \nlow/mid 90s.",
